// DF-172f: one rule, two positions, and until design 172 part 2 only one of
// them had a diagnostic. The REPEAT literal said cleanly what it would not
// accept; the TYPE annotation reached codegen with an unresolved length and
// died as `internal compiler error: Array type missing element type or size`.
//
// DF-172j then made the foldable half of that rule WORK — a `static
// REGION_SIZE: Int = 1024` is a length now (see `const_static_length.saw`) —
// so what is left to pin here is the half that is still refused, and the
// message that separates the two. An `unsafe static var` is mutable: its value
// is a fact about the running program, not about the source, so it can be no
// more a length than a `var` local can. The diagnostic names WHICH static and
// why, rather than reading as "a static may not be named here".
//
// DF-307a re-worded the hint's static clause, because "initialized by a plain
// integer literal" had stopped being true twice over: design 186 made a
// DERIVED static a constant (`static B: Int = A * 2` is a length), and DF-232g
// made an IMPORTED one fold too. The clause the hint owes is the one this file
// is here to teach — the static's own initializer has to fold — and a mutable
// one has no initializer to fold in that sense at all.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: array length is not a compile-time constant
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: the mutable static `ARENA_BYTES`
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: whose own initializer folds

unsafe static var ARENA_BYTES: Int = 1024

static ARENA: [UInt8; ARENA_BYTES] = [0; 1024]

func main() {
    print(ARENA[0])
}
